\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A bimodule metric needs both directions}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let the calculus be free on a digraph, over a commutative unital
ring #{k}. It admits a bimodule metric if and only if the digraph
is bidirected. A bimodule metric is an element of
#{\Omega\otimes_A\Omega} and an #{A}-bimodule pairing of that
tensor into #{A} whose two contractions recover the identity of
#{\Omega}. Every coefficient that appears is a unit. The field
case is \citet{Proposition 3.4}{majid2013graphs}. The forward-edge
torus admits no such metric. Whenever a weight and its reverse
are equal units, the support of the corresponding weighted
Laplacian is bidirected and the metric exists.}

\proof{
\p{The balanced tensor is the free #{k}-module on composable
pairs #{e_{xy}\otimes e_{yz}}. An #{A}-bimodule map from that
tensor to the functions sends #{e_{xy}\otimes e_{yz}} to a
function supported at #{x} and at #{z}. The two supports agree
only when #{x=z}, and the value is then a scalar times the
indicator of #{x}. Thus only a reverse pair pairs, and
##{\langle e_{xy},e_{yx}\rangle=c_{xy}\,\delta_x.}
Write a metric as the sum of terms #{\mu_{xy}\,e_{xy}\otimes e_{yx}}.
Pair a test form against the first factor. The only nonzero
value is #{\langle e_{yx},e_{xy}\rangle=c_{yx}\,\delta_y}, and the
second factor is then #{e_{yx}}. The product #{\mu_{xy}c_{yx}}
equals #{1} for every edge exactly when each reverse edge
exists and #{\mu_{xy}} is the inverse of #{c_{yx}}. Both
factors are then units. Pairing against the second factor
recovers the first factor by the same product.}

\p{Conversely, if the digraph is bidirected and each #{c_{xy}}
is a unit, set #{\mu_{xy}=c_{yx}^{-1}}. Both contractions are
the identity. The forward-edge torus has no reverse edge, so
the first paragraph forbids a metric. Equal unit weights in
the two directions give a bidirected support, and the same
converse supplies the metric.}
}
